    <DataNode elementId="AT1G67080-MONOMER" textLabel="neoxanthin synthase non-catalytic putative component" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q8LFP9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">The ABA4 protein appears to play a role in &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin production based on analyses of &lt;i&gt;aba4&lt;/i&gt; mutants. However, attempts to demonstrate enzymatic activity for ABA4 &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; were unsuccessful, and ABA4 does not possess any known enzymatically active domains |CITS: [17470058]|. It is therefore possible that ABA4 does not directly catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD1F-135 &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin|, or perhaps additional accessory proteins must interact with ABA4 to allow it to function &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [17470058]|. ABA4 does have four predicted transmembrane helices that indicate some potential structural similarity between ABA4 and the rhodopsin apoprotein which binds to the structurally-related compound |FRAME: CPD-881 11-cis-Retinal|, and &lt;i&gt;aba4&lt;/i&gt; mutants have elevated levels of |FRAME: CPD1F-133 &quot;&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-violaxanthin| and &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-violaxanthin, as well as reduced levels of |FRAME: CPD1F-135 &quot;&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin| and &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin |CITS: [17470058]|. Conversely, overexpression of ABA4 increases the levels of |FRAME: CPD1F-135 &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin| and &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-neoxanthin |CITS: [17470058]|. Nevertheless, no evidence of catalytic activity for ABA4 has been detected thus far |CITS: [17470058]|. ABA4 transcripts are expressed throughout Arabidopsis plants and do not change appreciably in response to drought stress, but mutant analyses indicate that ABA4 may play a role in protecting plants from water deficits |CITS: [17470058]|. Proteins similar to ABA4 have been identified by sequence homology in other plants and they all seem to possess chloroplast signal peptides. Putative orthologs in cyanobacteria that lack a signal peptide have also been found |CITS: [17470058]|. Further work will be required to determine if any of these proteins have neoxanthin synthase activity alone or in a novel enzymatic complex.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G67080-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G67080.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q8LFP9" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-17743" />
      <Property key="GOTerms" value="GO:0031969" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G67080.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G67080" />
